+# Signets signed with ML-DSA-44 only (`-signetpqchallenge`)
+
+`-signetpqchallenge=<pubkey>` makes a signet whose blocks only an ML-DSA-44
+(FIPS 204) key can sign. There is no classical signature anywhere in its block
+authorization. It is a separate network: its challenge, and so its network
+magic, differs from every other signet's.
+
+This is an experiment of the Ark-0 test network. It is not part of BIP 325 or
+any other BIP.
+
+## The challenge
+
+The option sets the challenge to the P2MR (BIP 360) output of the key: a script
+tree with the key's `OP_CHECKMLDSA44` leaf
+(`<pk[0]> <pk[1]> <pk[2]> OP_CHECKMLDSA44`, see `doc/p2mr-mldsa44.md`) and a
+leaf that can only fail (`OP_RETURN`), both at depth one:
+
+    OP_2 <TapBranch(TapLeaf(0xc0, pq_leaf), TapLeaf(0xc0, OP_RETURN))>
+
+Deriving the challenge from the key, rather than accepting any P2MR challenge,
+matters: a P2MR output whose tree is a single leaf is spent without running
+that leaf, so an arbitrary P2MR challenge would not say who may sign blocks.
+
+`-signetchallenge` may be given too, but only with the same value. The option
+cannot be combined with `-signetpqblock`. At startup a node logs the challenge
+it uses (`Signet with challenge ...`), which the operators of a network can
+compare.
+
+## The rules
+
+On a signet with the option:
+
+- The signet solution is checked with the P2MR and `OP_CHECKMLDSA44` script
+  rules added to the signet flags. It is an empty scriptSig and the witness
+  stack of the ML-DSA-44 leaf: five signature chunks, the leaf script and its
+  33-byte control block. The signature signs the leaf's signature message over
+  the BIP 325 virtual transaction, with the context string of
+  `OP_CHECKMLDSA44`.
+- The coinbase output that holds the witness commitment must be exactly
+  `OP_RETURN`, the commitment, and the signet solution push
+  (`ecc7daa2 ‖ solution`) in its shortest encoding, with nothing after it.
+  Otherwise anyone could re-encode the push or append bytes, which changes a
+  block's hash but not what its solution signs.
+- `OP_CHECKMLDSA44` is active in transactions from height 1.
+
+The genesis block has no solution, as on every signet.
+
+## Every node needs the option
+
+A node given the same challenge through `-signetchallenge` alone checks
+solutions with the old flags. Under those a P2MR program is an unknown witness
+version, so it accepts any solution, including none. Such a node offers no
+block authorization at all. Every node of such a network must run with
+`-signetpqchallenge`, and with software that knows it.
+
+## Producing blocks
+
+`test/functional/test_framework/signet_pq.py` builds the solution:
+`signet_p2mr_solution()` computes the virtual transactions as
+`contrib/signet/miner` does, signs the leaf's signature message and serializes
+the witness. Add it as the signet solution push, recompute the merkle root and
+grind the nonce. The solution is about 3.8 KB of non-witness data in the
+coinbase, so start the node that makes templates with
+`-blockreservedweight=24000` or more.
